              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Mechanism</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Incretin mimetic</p></list-item><list-item><p>Delays gastric emptying</p></list-item><list-item><p>Increases satiety</p></list-item><list-item><p>Glucose-dependent insulin secretion</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>FDA Approval</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>December 23, 2014 (adults); December 4, 2020 (pediatric &#x02265;12 years)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Indications</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Adults: BMI &#x02265;30 or BMI &#x02265;27 with comorbidities; Pediatric: Age &#x02265;12 years, with a weight &#x0003e;60 kg and obesity (BMI &#x02265;95th percentile for age and sex)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Dosing</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Week 1: 0.6 mg SC daily &#x02192; Week 2: 1.2 mg &#x02192; Week 3: 1.8 mg &#x02192; Week 4: 2.4 mg &#x02192; Week 5+: 3 mg (maximum dose)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Administration</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Subcutaneous injection once daily in the abdomen, thigh, or upper arm</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Contraindications</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>MEN2</p></list-item><list-item><p>Personal/family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Pregnancy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypersensitivity</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Common Adverse Effects</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Nausea (39%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Diarrhea (21%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Constipation (19%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Vomiting (16%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Injection site reactions</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Serious but Infrequent Adverse Effects</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Pancreatitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Gallbladder disease</p></list-item><list-item><p>Thyroid tumors</p></list-item><list-item><p>Kidney injury</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypoglycemia</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Monitoring</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Weight</p></list-item><list-item><p>BMI</p></list-item><list-item><p>Blood glucose (if diabetic)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Lipase</p></list-item><list-item><p>Renal function</p></list-item><list-item><p>Thyroid symptoms</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Efficacy</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>5.4% to 8.0% weight loss at 56 weeks</p></list-item><list-item><p>63.2% achieve &#x02265;5% weight loss</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Storage</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Refrigerate unopened</p></list-item><list-item><p>After first use: 30 days at room temperature or refrigerated</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Phentermine-topiramate Extended-Release</bold>
</p>
        <p>The combination therapy&#x000a0;phentermine-topiramate extended-release (Qsymia) leverages complementary mechanisms through phentermine's sympathomimetic appetite suppression and topiramate's complex neurological effects on appetite and metabolism (see <bold>Table 4</bold>).&#x000a0;Please&#x000a0;refer to StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/120320">Pharmacologic Therapy for Obesity</ext-link>," for additional&#x000a0;information.<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic>The CONQUER and SEQUEL trials demonstrated 6.6% to 8.6% weight loss at 56 weeks, with 62% to 70% of participants achieving 5% weight loss or more. Long-term safety data from the SEQUEL study confirmed sustained efficacy for up to 2 years, with an acceptable tolerability profile.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-179763.table3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 4. Phentermine-Topiramate Clinical Profile</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Parameters</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Drug Information</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Trade Name</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Qsymia</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35.8184px;">
                <td style="height: 35.8184px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Generic Name</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35.8184px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Phentermine-topiramate extended release</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Drug Class</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Sympathomimetic/anticonvulsant combination</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Mechanism</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Phentermine: appetite suppression</p></list-item><list-item><p>Topiramate: multiple mechanisms (eg, gamma-aminobutyric acid, glutamate)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>FDA Approval</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>July 17, 2012 (adults)</p></list-item><list-item><p>June 27, 2022 (pediatric &#x02265;12 years)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Indications</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Adults: BMI &#x02265;30 or BMI &#x02265;27 with comorbidities</p></list-item><list-item><p>Pediatric: &#x02265;12 years with obesity (BMI &#x02265;95th percentile for age and sex)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Dosing</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Start with 3.75 mg/23 mg daily for 14 days, then increase to 7.5 mg/46 mg daily, and continue to increase to a maximum total daily dose of 15 mg/92 mg daily if necessary.</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Administration</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Oral morning dosing, avoid evening&#x000a0;administration</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 61px;">
                <td style="height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Contraindications</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 61px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Pregnancy, glaucoma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hyperthyroidism</p></list-item><list-item><p>Monoamine oxidase inhibitors, unstable cardiovascular disease</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 74px;">
                <td style="height: 74px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Common Adverse Effects</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 74px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Paresthesia (21%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Dry mouth (19%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Constipation (17%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Insomnia (11%)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 74px;">
                <td style="height: 74px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Serious but Infrequent Adverse Effects</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 74px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Metabolic acidosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Kidney stones</p></list-item><list-item><p>Acute angle-closure glaucoma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cognitive impairment</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 87px;">
                <td style="height: 87px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Monitoring</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 87px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Blood pressure</p></list-item><list-item><p>Heart rate</p></list-item><list-item><p>Electrolytes</p></list-item><list-item><p>Creatinine</p></list-item><list-item><p>Pregnancy test monthly for women</p></list-item><list-item><p>BMI</p></list-item><list-item><p>Weight</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Efficacy</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>6.6% to 8.6% weight loss at 56 weeks</p></list-item><list-item><p>62% to 70% achieve &#x02265;5% weight loss</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 48px;">
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Special Notes</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 48px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Risk evaluation and mitigation strategies program</p></list-item><list-item><p>Contraindicated in pregnancy due to tetratogenicity (oral clefts): prescribe only to women of childbearing potential who are using reliable contraception.</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Naltrexone-bupropion Extended Release</bold>
</p>
        <p>Naltrexone-bupropion extended-release (Contrave) targets the neurological aspects of obesity by affecting reward pathways and appetite regulation (see <bold>Table 5</bold>).&#x000a0;The synergistic interaction of naltrexone's opioid receptor blockade and bupropion's monoamine reuptake inhibition modulates food cravings and hedonic eating behaviors. The COR clinical trial program demonstrated a 5% to 6% weight loss at 56 weeks, with 42% to 50% of participants achieving a weight loss of 5% or greater.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-179763.table4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 5. Naltrexone-Bupropion Clinical Profile</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Parameters</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Drug Information</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Trade Name</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Contrave</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Generic Name</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Naltrexone HCl/bupropion HCl extended-release</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Drug Class</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Opioid antagonist/atypical antidepressant combination</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Mechanism</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Naltrexone: Opioid receptor blockade</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bupropion: Dopamine/norepinephrine reuptake inhibition</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>FDA Approval</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>September 10, 2014</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Indications</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>BMI &#x02265;30</p></list-item><list-item><p>BMI &#x02265;27 with weight-related comorbidities</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Dosing</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Week 1: 8 mg/90 mg daily</p></list-item><list-item><p>Week 2: 8 mg/90 mg twice daily</p></list-item><list-item><p>Week 3: 16 mg/90 mg in the morning plus 8 mg/90 mg in the evening</p></list-item><list-item><p>Week 4 and beyond: 16 mg/90 mg twice daily</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Administration</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Taken orally with food (not crushed, chewed, or divided)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Contraindications</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Seizure disorders</p></list-item><list-item><p>Eating disorders</p></list-item><list-item><p>Chronic opioid use</p></list-item><list-item><p>Abrupt alcohol/benzodiazepine discontinuation</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Common Adverse Effects</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Nausea (32%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Constipation (19%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Headache (18%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Vomiting (11%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Dizziness (10%)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Serious Adverse Effects</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Seizures</p></list-item><list-item><p>Angle-closure glaucoma</p></list-item><list-item><p>Increased blood pressure and heart rate</p></list-item><list-item><p>Suicidal thoughts</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Monitoring</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Blood pressure</p></list-item><list-item><p>Heart rate</p></list-item><list-item><p>Mood changes</p></list-item><list-item><p>Seizure risk</p></list-item><list-item><p>Weight</p></list-item><list-item><p>BMI</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Efficacy</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>5% to 6% weight loss at 56 weeks</p></list-item><list-item><p>42% to 50% achieve &#x02265;5% weight loss</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Special Notes</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Black box warning for suicide risk</p></list-item><list-item><p>Contraindicated with opioids</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Orlistat&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Orlistat (Xenical/Alli) remains the only FDA-approved obesity medication available over-the-counter (OTC), providing patients with accessibility without requiring a prescription (see <bold>Table 6</bold>). Orlistat's unique mechanism of lipase inhibition provides local gastrointestinal effects without systemic absorption, offering a favorable safety profile for patients with multiple comorbidities. Clinical trials demonstrate an additional weight loss of 2 to 3 kg beyond diet and exercise, with 37% of participants achieving a weight loss of 5% or more.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-179763.table5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 6. Orlistat Clinical Profile</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Parameters</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Drug Information</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Trade Name</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Xenical (120 mg), Alli (60 mg OTC)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Generic Name</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Orlistat</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Drug Class</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Lipase inhibitor</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Mechanism</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Reversible inhibition of gastric and pancreatic lipases</p></list-item><list-item><p>Reduces fat absorption by approximately 30%</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>FDA Approval</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>April 26, 1999 (Xenical)</p></list-item><list-item><p>February 7, 2007 (Alli OTC)</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Indications</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Xenical: BMI &#x02265;30 or BMI &#x02265;27 with comorbidities</p></list-item><list-item><p>Alli: BMI &#x02265;25</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Dosing</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Xenical: 120 mg 3 times daily with meals</p></list-item><list-item><p>Alli: 60 mg 3 times daily with fat-containing meals</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Administration</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Orally with meals, skip the dose if the meal contains no fat</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Contraindications</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Chronic malabsorption</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cholestasis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Pregnancy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Breastfeeding</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Common Adverse Effects</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Oily stools (27%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Fecal urgency (22%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Fatty fecal evacuation (20%)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Flatus with discharge</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Serious Adverse Effects</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Severe liver injury (rare)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Kidney stones</p></list-item><list-item><p>Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Monitoring</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Liver function</p></list-item><list-item><p>Fat-soluble vitamin status (A, D, E, K)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Weight</p></list-item><list-item><p>BMI</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bowel habits</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Efficacy</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>2&#x02013;3 kg additional weight loss beyond diet</p></list-item><list-item><p>37% achieve &#x02265;5% weight loss</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Special Notes</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Only OTC obesity medication</p></list-item><list-item><p>Local gastrointestinal effects</p></list-item><list-item><p>No systemic absorption</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Specialized Medications</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Setmelanotide&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Setmelanotide (IMCIVREE)<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>represents precision medicine in obesity treatment, specifically targeting rare genetic forms of obesity through melanocortin-4 receptor agonism (see <bold>Table 7</bold>). This highly specialized medication addresses the underlying pathophysiology of monogenic obesity disorders by restoring hypothalamic appetite regulation pathways disrupted by genetic mutations in the proopiomelanocortin pathway.</p>
